modelAnswer:
Henri Fayol (1841–1925), a French mining engineer and managing director of a mining company (Commentry-Fourchambault), is regarded as the father of modern management theory (administrative or management process theory). His book General and Industrial Management (Administration Industrielle et Générale, 1916) set out his ideas.

1. Classification of business activities: technical (production), commercial (buying and selling), financial (raising and using capital), security (protection of property and people), accounting, and managerial activities.

2. The functions (elements) of management: Fayol identified five: planning (forecasting), organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling. These are still the basis of the management process (POSDCORB, Koontz's functions).

3. Fourteen principles of management:
(1) Division of work (specialisation);
(2) Authority and responsibility;
(3) Discipline;
(4) Unity of command (one boss for each employee);
(5) Unity of direction (one plan for activities with the same objective);
(6) Subordination of individual interest to the general interest;
(7) Remuneration (fair pay);
(8) Centralisation (the right balance);
(9) Scalar chain (line of authority, with the 'gang plank' for direct horizontal communication);
(10) Order (a place for everything and everyone);
(11) Equity (fairness and kindness);
(12) Stability of tenure of personnel;
(13) Initiative;
(14) Esprit de corps (team spirit).

4. Universality of management: management principles apply to all kinds of organisations (business, government, religious, military), not only industry.

5. Management can be taught: Fayol argued that management is a skill that can be learnt and should be taught in schools and colleges.

6. Qualities of managers: he listed the physical, mental, moral, educational, technical and experiential qualities managers need.

Assessment: Fayol gave management a systematic framework from the top-management perspective, complementing Taylor's shop-floor scientific management. Critics say his principles are too rigid and neglect human and environmental factors, but they remain influential.
